A||||19||Mach10||440t4... 40t60.... ARRRRRGGG!||||11||
Z||000000||Mach10||03-27-2001||10:48 PM||||I have searched through the entire forum, and found LOTS of argument about changing the th125 trans to a 4 speed. Unfortunately, I mostly only come up with instructions to search for various trans model types. Fair enough, it's been covered... I do that, and find some information, but am still stuck.<P>Simply put: I want to switch from a 3-spd Auto in an 86GT to a 4 speed. Standard 2.8 for now. Which, of all the options, is:<BR>a) Easiest<BR>b) Most worth while<BR>c) Most appropriate for a "first timer" in the world of part swapping(bracket fabrication etc)...<P>Thanks a lot, people!<BR>Sean||||reg||11
Z||000001||rockcrawl||03-28-2001||05:55 PM||||The 440t4 and the 4t60 are pretty much the same. I don't know the actual differences, but the two I have look identical except that the caddy has the Fiero type VSS and the olds has the later type VSS. Both of mine are 1987. I'd look for one with the Fiero style VSS to save some headaches there. Any GM FWD 4spd from whenever they started making them ('85?) until they went electronic ('91?) will work just as well. They all use the same axles. they all use the same mounts as far as I can tell, nothing custom just drill some holes. They all need the same shift cable bracket mods. With a 2.8 you can just use the Fiero shift arm and flip it around instead of extending it or making a reverser mechanism like with a 3800 or 4.9. You'll need to make an extra notch in the shifter so you can select first gear. It will be a pretty simple swap.  <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||03-29-2001||10:18 AM||||Jon's advice was right on the mark.  My 2 cents is of course to get as low mileage of a unit as you can find.  One clarification on Jon's post is that all the axles for the 4spds are the same but differ from the tmh125 so count on getting an axle set for it.  Also some cars mounted the eng trans differently than the Fiero so when you're at the salvage yard get the same style brackets as the Fiero.  I put a 4T60E in one of my cars and had to drill on the back side of the cradle.  I don't think that is the case with the 440 on non-electric 4T60.||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||03-29-2001||06:05 PM||||I had to drill on the back side of the cradle for the '87 4T60 Oldsmobile trans using Olds brackets and Fiero mounts. The '93 4T60E I just did was exactly the same, but I used an '87 cadilac 4T60 bracket on the back side. The newer cars use a one piece integrated bracket/rubber mount. The front one works fine, but the rear needs to be the older fiero style bracket. Basicly I think the brackets from any non-electronic four speed will work. All the one's I've seen are the same.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mach10||03-29-2001||07:02 PM||||Thanks for the info, guys!<BR>Are there any issues that need to be dealt with when using an electronic tranny? I'm looking for a quick-and-dirty swap, keeping as much original as possible.<BR>Am I correct in guessing that a 4t60e tranny does not get data from the ECM? Does it use a remote switch to toggle the overdrive ratio? The lock-up converter should just patch in to the stock wiring... correct? <BR>I'm assuming that I will need to wire my own "overdrive" switch for an electronic transmission? Do I have to make any changes (other than the speedo) to the fiero ECM/Electrical system?||||reg||6
Z||000005||rockcrawl||03-29-2001||07:37 PM||||The electronic ones need an ECM to control them. The Fiero ECM will not do it. You definately need to get a non-electronic one. The Fiero wiring doesn't need to be modified. Any four speed before 1991 should work.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mach10||03-29-2001||07:42 PM||||Ok, so to clarify:<BR>-Easiest swap would be a 4-spd NON electronic transmission. <BR>-Cradles should match up fairly close, requiring maybe some drilling, cutting.<BR>-No ECM or wiring changes<P>Correct? Assuming so, how is the shifting accomplished? Am I going to keep the PRND21 pattern, with no additions, or will it be more like PRN432 with no low-1st ratio? OR, will I have to add a little "overdrive" switch? That'd be my last question, unless I can think of more  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Sean<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000007||rockcrawl||03-29-2001||10:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>-Easiest swap would be a 4-spd NON electronic transmission. <BR>-Cradles should match up fairly close, requiring maybe some drilling, cutting.<BR>-No ECM or wiring changes<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's correct. You're shift pattern will be PRN432 unless you grind or file another detent notch on the shifter mechanism. Actually I was just working on a shifter tonight that already had the extra notch. Was GM thinking about putting a four speed in the Fiero?! The last one I did needed an extra one added.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||03-29-2001||11:03 PM||||Hey Rockcrawl: Don't suppose you could elaborate a little on that, could you? On my Jag, the "click" action and stops are from the tranny itself. does the Fiero ratchet on it's own, and just run out of room for full PRND321, or does the shift-lever itself have a mechanical action, that just needs to be extended?<BR>On a scale of 1-10, with one being as hard as changing brake pads, and 10 rebuilding a transmission, how difficult would it be to modify the stock shifter?<BR>Sean||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||03-29-2001||11:07 PM||||I think you will only be able to go down to 3 with the stock lever without cutting out another 1/4" of the detent plate. <P>Also, the pattern is backwards unless you can swap the trans lever over to the other side. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||grinthock||05-28-2001||12:20 AM||||I know I might be bumping this subject up a fair ways... But I am considering this too, has anyone who has done this seen a large performance improvement?  Or just gas milage?  <P>Is this something that could be done WITHOUT a lift?  I don't have the facilities to fabricate stuff as I live in an apt building, and have limited space.||||reg||1
Z||000011||KRMFiero||05-28-2001||06:42 AM||||I dont think i saw this mentioned,  to bolt the 440T directly in with no mods:<P>buy a Trany mount for a 1989 Pontiac 6000 2.8 Automatic overdrive<P>if you are going to have the engine out, buying a caddy tranny why not just put the whole engine/trans right in?  <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx</A> <P>Kyle<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||p4n1c||05-28-2001||08:10 AM||||The TH440T4 and 4T60 are exactly the same, they just renamed it in the '89 model year.  I guess they wanted to move away from Turbo Hydromatic in the naming, even though they have been named that way for a long time(TH400, TH350, etc).<P>If the 440 will bolt on directly, the 4T60 should too.  Just make sure it's not a 4T60-E that's a totally different transmission(electronic).<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<BR>2.8L MPFI V6/TH440-T4 OD auto<BR>"Ride the geriatric mobile.. err..  rocket"||||reg||1
Z||000013||grinthock||05-28-2001||10:12 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KRMFiero:<BR><B>I dont think i saw this mentioned,  to bolt the 440T directly in with no mods:<P>buy a Trany mount for a 1989 Pontiac 6000 2.8 Automatic overdrive<P>if you are going to have the engine out, buying a caddy tranny why not just put the whole engine/trans right in?  <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx</A> <P>Kyle<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>The engine is still in the car.  I wonder how long it would take to have it done by the fiero shop...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||SteveJ||05-28-2001||11:56 AM||||I bought a 440T4 a month and a half ago at a junk yard for $225. It takes about 2 gallons to fill from scratch regardless of what the book says (in fact I think mine was a little more). They are figuring a partial change as in filter replacement. You gotta fill it, start it and fill again. I bought those gallon jugs at Kmart. <P>Also it has a vacume modulator that regulates shift quality as in soft(slipping) or harsh(self destruction). If you have poor shifts the modulator is only $10 at Autozone and the replacement is adjustable. I had two bad OEM modualtor around here. Couldn't beleive both were bad. New one really helped.<P>After you know the modualtor is OK then set the TV cable by the book. Setting it before doesn't help, I know (now).||||reg||1
Z||000015||Formula88||05-28-2001||01:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>Hey Rockcrawl: Don't suppose you could elaborate a little on that, could you? On my Jag, the "click" action and stops are from the tranny itself. does the Fiero ratchet on it's own, and just run out of room for full PRND321, or does the shift-lever itself have a mechanical action, that just needs to be extended?<BR>On a scale of 1-10, with one being as hard as changing brake pads, and 10 rebuilding a transmission, how difficult would it be to modify the stock shifter?<BR>Sean</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The detent does come from the tranny, but the 3 speed shifter doesn't have enough range to move far enough to get 1st gear.  So you need the notch to allow the shifter to move a little bit farther.  I had the same problem in my '69 Firebird when I switched from a 2-speed auto to a 3-speed.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||grinthock||05-28-2001||02:44 PM||||MY question still hasnt' really been answered, could I do this myself?  How long would it take?<P>Thanks everyone||||reg||1
Z||000017||KRMFiero||05-28-2001||07:41 PM||||Could you do it yourself? Yes<BR>How long should it take? My guess is 3 days, take your time though you dont want to break anything.||||reg||1
Z||000018||88formula||05-28-2001||08:45 PM||||I had an 89 Bonneville with the Turbo hydromatic 440-T4 transmission. It had a 2.93 final drive ratio and a 1200 stall converter. The stock 2.8-liter engine with the three-speed automatic (3.18 final drive) will go 0-60 mph in 8.7 seconds and run the quarter mile in 16.6 seconds @ 85.8 mph. If you swapped the 440-T4 transmission with a 2.93 final drive and lower stall converter into your fiero than you would go 0-60 mph in 9.4 seconds and run the quarter mile in 17 seconds @ 86.4 mph.<P>The only benefit I see you getting is more highway mpg with the overdrive. You could get way better performance and gas mileage if you swapped in a five speed manual transmission. Than your car would go 0-60 mph in 8 seconds and run the quarter mile in 16 seconds and get 30 mpg on the highway like mine does. After owning my five-speed formula and driving an automatic fiero I could never happily own an automatic fiero. It’s just not as much fun! <P><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 05-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||grinthock||05-28-2001||10:25 PM||||ahh performance numbers speak....<P>-- forget it.<P><BR>Thanks!||||reg||1
